# Chapter 10: the IDE, by what its actions leave behind
#
# The tool window, the editor and the run configuration are views over the daemon: whatever you click sends one of the operations
# you have already used in this tour, and the result is a file or a state you can read here. This chapter is the walk of the IDE
# (docs/demo-m3.md section 9 and docs/walkthrough.md parts 6-13), told as scenario steps: each one names the IDE action that sends
# the same operation, runs it, and asserts what the action leaves behind. The clicking itself is done in the sandbox IDE
# (`bb tour:ide`, or `bb ide:run --ws <ws> --home <home>`); chapters 1-9 have to have run (the estate is up, a session is open).
# @env local

### shell paths
# Terraform operations take the root directory; ask the shell where this checkout is.
# @cwd ..
# @capture root = out
pwd | tr -d '\n'

### shell workspace
# @capture ws = out
printf %s "$DLB_WS"

# Tool window -> Services. The rows are `service.list`; the toolbar's Stop, Start and Restart send `service.stop`, `service.start` and
# `service.restart` for the selected row. The tab renders the daemon's answer and refreshes itself every 15 seconds while it is the
# tab you look at: stop A from a terminal and its row says `stopped` without a click.

### op service.stop services-tab-stop
# Stop keeps the container (a Start brings the same one back).
# @assert $.status == stopped
{"name": "a"}

### op service.list services-tab-rows
# The row the tab shows for a: mode local, stopped. Rows for a2 and b follow in name order.
# @assert $[0].name == a
# @assert $[0].mode == local
# @assert $[0].status == stopped
{}

### op service.start services-tab-start
# @assert $.status == running
# @assert $.container == deliberator-{{ws}}-service-a
{"name": "a"}

### op service.restart services-tab-restart
# @assert $.status == running
{"name": "a"}

# Run | "Deliberator Service: a" -> Run starts the service and follows its log into the console: ONE `logs.fetch` follow for the
# whole Run session, which ends as one read-only file in the session's logs/ (a hard link of the raw blob). Stop (or the red
# square) stops the follow and the service; a Stop pressed while the start is still going waits for the start and stops what it made.

### op logs.fetch run-console-log
# The one-shot form of what the console shows (the follow is the same operation with `for`/follow).
# @assert $.lines > 0
# @capture log = $.path
{"target": "log/service-a", "since": "2m"}

### shell log-is-read-only
# A finished log is read-only (mode 444) and shares its inode with the raw blob (link count 2).
# @assert out contains 444 2
stat -c '%a %h' "{{log}}"

# AWS tab. Every file the explorer produces is written by the daemon (`explore.write`, which names the place under the workspace's
# project directory); the tab opens the path it gets. Double-clicking a resource appends a read step to explore/explore-<env>.scn,
# created once with a header: a second `if-missing` write leaves the file as you edited it.

### op explore.write explorer-scenario
# (`created` and `written` say whether this run made the file or found it from an earlier one.)
# @assert $.path matches /\/explore\/explore-local\.scn$/
# @capture explore = $.path
{"name": "explore-local.scn", "text": "# Written by the AWS explorer: one read step per resource you opened. Edit, rerun, keep.\n# @env local\n", "if-missing": true}

### op explore.write explorer-scenario-again
# The file exists now: nothing is written, the same path comes back.
# @assert $.created == false
# @assert $.written == false
# @assert $.path == {{explore}}
{"name": "explore-local.scn", "text": "replaced?\n", "if-missing": true}

### op explore.write path-is-the-daemons
# The name is a name, never a path: `../` and friends are folded into one file name under explore/.
# @assert $.path matches /\/explore\/tour-chapter10\.txt$/
{"name": "../../tour-chapter10.txt", "text": "written by chapter 10\n"}

### op terraform.local ask-the-variables
# Apply asks first: the tab fetches the variables the daemon derives for this workspace and shows them in the confirmation
# ("terraform apply of <root> onto environment local? Variables passed (-var): ..."), all off the UI thread.
# @assert $.vars.container_endpoint == http://deliberator-{{ws}}-floci:4566
# @assert $.vars.service_a_url == http://deliberator-{{ws}}-service-a:8081
{"dir": "{{root}}/estate/terraform/floci", "action": "vars"}

### op terraform.local plan-from-the-tab
# Plan sends no variables of its own: the daemon derives the same ones, so on the estate's own root it plans nothing.
# @timeout 5m
# @assert $.summary contains No changes
{"dir": "{{root}}/estate/terraform/floci", "action": "plan"}

### op terraform.drift drift-from-the-tab
# Terraform drift...: declared vs. existing; the tab marks the tree and writes the report through `explore.write`.
# @assert $.missing == []
# @assert $.matching > 10
{"dir": "{{root}}/estate/terraform/floci"}

### op terraform.local no-root
# A directory without Terraform files is an error the button can show, never a crash of the tab.
# @assert error.type == tf/no-root
{"dir": "{{root}}/docs", "action": "plan"}

# Config navigation: Ctrl+click on ${TABLE_NAME} in application.properties offers the template line, the Terraform declaration and
# the parameter's view; the view's banner says how old the snapshot is, and Refresh (the banner's link, or Deliberator | Refresh
# Config Snapshot) fetches a new one and the banner says "0 s ago". A deleted parameter turns the placeholder into a warning.

### op config.refresh banner-refresh
# @assert $.parameters > 30
# @assert $.secrets == 2
# @capture fetched = $.fetched-at
{"env": "local"}

### op config.chain config-chain
# What Deliberator | Config Chain prints, and what the inspection reads: five entries for a, none broken, TABLE_NAME in line 7 of
# application.properties with its parameter present.
# @assert $.entries[0].placeholder == TABLE_NAME
# @assert $.entries[0].line == 7
# @assert $.entries[0].parameter.status == present
# @assert $.broken == []
# @assert $.snapshot.fetched-at == {{fetched}}
{"name": "a", "env": "local"}

# Secrets checkbox: "Take secret values from environment" in the run configuration is `service.start` with `secrets-from`; the IDE
# shows each of the daemon's two confirmations as a dialog. Cancel at either one is the typed `service/cancelled`, nothing started.

### op service.start secrets-confirmation-1
# The first confirmation: what would be read, and a token for step 2. (Chapter 7 gives both and looks inside the container.)
# @assert error.type == confirm-required
{"name": "a", "secrets-from": "local2"}

### op service.status a-is-still-plain
# Nothing was started or recreated by asking.
# @assert $[0].status == running
{"name": "a"}

# Topology..., the Services tab's globe button, is `topology.set` and then the balloon's "Apply now" is `runtime.ensure` (chapter 6).
# Debug attaches IntelliJ's debugger to the service's JDWP port and Stop detaches: the container keeps running. Logging breakpoints
# ("Add Logpoint") print into the debug console without stopping it. Those three are editor and debugger features: the IDE tests and
# the walk in .scratch/walk check them; what is left behind is the running service:

### op service.list services-after-the-walk
# @assert $[0].name == a
# @assert $[0].status == running
{}
